- Structure (the concepts, steps, options, components): one node each, with a short label.
- What the content says about one of them (how it works, why, conditions, examples, caveats): in the content's own words, in the body of its "text" node — or, for a "shape" (which holds only a label), in its own "text" node linked from it with {"from":"<concept id>","to":"<detail id>","type":"explained_by"}. Never folded into the concept node's label, and never added as another step of a flow.
- Long background, examples or code: a "page" node (or "code" node) linked the same way.
- A list item that only introduces a nested list ("**Example:**", "**Significance:**", "**Key challenges:**" followed by sub-bullets) is ONE unit with its sub-bullets: keep the label and every sub-bullet together in the same "text" node — the label as its bold first line (or its "title"), the sub-bullets as bullets beneath it. Never a node holding only the label, and never its sub-bullets as separate nodes away from it: a reader must see which label each point belongs to. The same goes for a bold label with its own items in a list (e.g. "**What it is:** …" / "**Significance:** …" / "**Example:** …" of one concept): they are the parts of ONE concept's node, not separate cards.
A table in the content stays a "table" with every row and every cell's full text (numbers, names, paths, conditions) — never a shortened copy. You may add a visual of it (a flow, a tree) beside the table, but not instead of it.
A link between steps of a sequence goes from the earlier step to the later one ("flows_to"), so arrows follow the order of the steps. Use "relates_to" only for an association with no direction or order.

The content below is numbered block by block: [T1], [T2] … tables, [L1] … lists, [C1] … code, [P1] … paragraphs. To place a block exactly as it is, use {"id":"n9","type":"source","ref":"T1"} (it takes "title", "importance" and links like any node) instead of retyping it: the app inserts the whole block, word for word. Place every table this way, with all its rows and columns — never a shortened copy — and any list, code or paragraph you would otherwise copy. Place each block at most once. The numbers are for placing blocks only: never write "[P1]", "[T1]" or the like in your own text. Never restate a block you placed: no shape, card or node repeating its rows, items or sentences — the block already shows them, and a second copy only crowds the canvas. Your own nodes are for what the content doesn't already show: structure the blocks don't have (shapes and links for a flow, a tree, dependencies between items) and your analysis.

Then analyse it — the reader must understand it at a glance, not only find its details:
- "metadata.takeaway": the section's main point in one line of your own words (at most 15 words), e.g. "Merge v3 now; two decisions wait on the engineer". Always give one.
- For a status or hand-off note: what is done, pending, blocked, and what needs a decision or someone's action (whose) — one line per item the content names (D1, D2 …, each request). Otherwise: the main point and what the reader should do with it.
- Put this analysis in "text" nodes marked "origin":"analysis" (e.g. {"id":"a1","type":"text","origin":"analysis","title":"Status","content":{"markdown":"..."}}), linked to what it is about where that helps. It may state what the content implies but doesn't say; it must never contradict the content or add facts it doesn't contain.
- The takeaway and analysis are specific to this content: name its own things (its checks, files, numbers, decisions). Never add a generic benefit or outcome it doesn't state ("improves user experience", "increases efficiency", "enhances reliability"); when the content gives no outcome, say what changed or what is open instead.
- The content's own details stay as they are above (in its own words, nothing left out). Analysis is added on top, never instead of them.

An earlier plan of this section fell short of an analysis: it lacked takeaway and analysis. This time give "metadata.takeaway" and "text" nodes marked "origin":"analysis" that cover all of it, as described above, together with every detail.

Content to visualize:

## Retries

[P1] Retries can improve reliability when the cache holds a valid result. However, they must not exceed 3 attempts.

[T1] | Case | Before | Now |
|---|---|---|
| Timeout | dropped | retried up to 3 times |

[L1] - Use an idempotency key
- Log every retry

[C1] \`\`\`ts
retry(3);
\`\`\`"
`;

